+## Separate pre-migration behavioral capture
+
+`behavior-baseline.json` contains five synthetic request/response pairs captured
+from main `42189a223fd44c823ca11aec689ea67b11c25790` before the migration.
+It records the original source/lock hashes, Rust version, target and capture
+binary hash. These values describe historical behavior; they are not independent
+evidence that the producer is scientifically correct.
+
+Reproduce in a separate checkout of that exact revision. Build the native core
+with `cargo build --locked -p innovate-core --lib`, then compile
+`capture_baseline.rs` against that resulting `libinnovate_core.rlib` with
+`rustc --edition 2024`, `--extern innovate_core=<rlib>` and
+`-L dependency=<target>/debug/deps`. Feed each recorded `request` as one JSON
+line on stdin; compare each resulting response with its corresponding
+`expected`. The original capture executable was equivalent to this small runner.
+
+Same-target exact equality was checked before and after the quantile migration.
+The portable regression keeps draws, acceptance counts, metadata and discrete
+grids/intervals exact. Grid masses use the existing `1e-12` absolute tolerance
+and posterior means the existing `1e-10` tolerance, because platform math
+libraries may differ in the last bits of `exp`/`ln`. A local pass does not
+qualify the unexecuted Linux/macOS/Windows matrix.
+
+Bootstrap captures remain available for inspection. The regression independently
+checks type-7 percentile coefficients using actual returned estimates, retaining
+the eight-success contract for these well-conditioned fixtures. It does not
+freeze solver estimates or confer acceptance on the separate linalg migration.
+
 ## Primary references
 
 - [mpmath inverse error function and error-function definitions](https://mpmath.org/doc/1.3.0/functions/expintegrals.html#erfinv)
